<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. Pray the Our Father as if for the first time</h4>
<p>Take ten minutes. Sit in silence for one minute first, to quiet your heart. Then say the Our Father very slowly, phrase by phrase. Pause after each phrase for a full breath. If a word or phrase catches your attention, stay there longer. When you finish, write which phrase impacted you most and why.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>B. Experience all five forms of prayer in one sitting</h4>
<p>Set aside fifteen minutes. Spend three minutes on each form of prayer: (1) Adoration — praise God simply for who he is, using Psalm 103 if you need words. (2) Petition — bring your own deepest need to God. (3) Intercession — pray for three specific people. (4) Thanksgiving — name five things you are grateful for today. (5) Praise — close with a psalm of pure praise (try Ps 150). Write which form felt most alive and which felt most difficult.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>C. Meditate with a psalm using lectio divina</h4>
<p>Choose Psalm 63 (&laquo;My soul thirsts for you&raquo;). Read it slowly four times: (1) <em>Lectio</em> — read, and notice what word or phrase stands out. (2) <em>Meditatio</em> — chew on that word; what does it mean? (3) <em>Oratio</em> — speak to God about what you discover. (4) <em>Contemplatio</em> — simply rest in God's presence, without words. Write what you experienced at each step.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>D. Design your prayer space</h4>
<p>Jesus says: &laquo;When you pray, go into your room, close the door and pray to your Father, who is unseen&raquo; (Mt 6:6). Do you have a place set apart for prayer? If not, create one this week. It can be very simple: a corner with a candle, a crucifix or icon, a comfortable chair. Describe the space you will use. What image or object will help you turn your heart to God?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>E. Listen to Taizé music for ten minutes</h4>
<p>Search online for &laquo;Taizé chant&raquo; (&laquo;Jesus, Remember Me,&raquo; &laquo;Stay with Me,&raquo; or &laquo;Ubi Caritas&raquo;). Sit in silence, listen, and let the repeated phrases sink into your heart. Do not analyze; simply receive. Afterward, sit in silence for three minutes. Write what you experienced: what did the music do to your soul?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>F. Keep a prayer journal for one week</h4>
<p>Each day, write briefly: what you said to God, how you felt, what Scripture passage accompanied you, and how the rest of the day went. At the end of the week, read back over everything you wrote. Do you notice any patterns? Any graces you would have missed? Write your summary here.</p>
